    "teacher_markdown": "The bug is a boundary error — chance_of_rain < 60 excludes 60 itself, so a value of exactly 60 falls through to the else branch and renders as rainy instead of overcast. Students may not think to test the boundary value itself (60) unless prompted; if they’re stuck, suggest they try a few different numbers for chance_of_rain — especially 20 and 60 exactly — and compare what scene appears against what the instructions say should appear at that value. \r\n\r\nThis is the same category of fix as the boundary error in the grade calculator from Investigate & Modify, so it’s a good moment to connect back to that lesson if students did it: < versus <= only disagrees at the exact boundary number, which is exactly why it’s easy to miss with casual testing.",
    "display_name": "Rain Display",
    "bubble_choice_description": "Fix a sky display that’s showing the wrong color for the chance of rain.",
    "mini_app": "theater",
    "mini_app_variant": "functional",
    "thumbnail_url": "https://images.code.org/c7b249f762e26f6297c882ba54370c5c-Screenshot 2026-11-12 101936.png",
    "validation_file": {
      "id": "1",
      "name": "test.py",
      "contents": "import re\nfrom unittest import TestCase\n\n\nPROVIDED_MARKER = \"# --- Provided: scene-drawing, do not edit ---\"\nASSIGNMENT_PATTERN = re.compile(r\"^chance_of_rain\\s*=\\s*\\d+\\s*$\", re.MULTILINE)\n\n\nclass TestDebugSkyDisplay(TestCase):\n    @classmethod\n    def setUpClass(cls):\n        \"\"\"Isolate the scene-logic portion of the source, above the provided\n        drawing functions, so we can re-run just the logic with different\n        chance_of_rain values -- without ever triggering a draw or play().\"\"\"\n        with open(\"main.py\") as f:\n            source = f.read()\n        cls.logic_snippet = source.split(PROVIDED_MARKER)[0] if PROVIDED_MARKER in source else None\n\n    def _scene_for(self, chance_of_rain):\n        snippet, count = ASSIGNMENT_PATTERN.subn(f\"chance_of_rain = {chance_of_rain}\", self.logic_snippet, count=1)\n        if count == 0:\n            self.fail(\n                \"We couldn't find a line matching `chance_of_rain = <number>` to test different values against. \"\n                \"Make sure chance_of_rain is still set with a plain number assignment.\"\n            )\n        namespace = {}\n        exec(compile(snippet, \"main.py\", \"exec\"), namespace)\n        return namespace.get(\"scene\")\n\n    def test_scene_matches_chance_of_rain_band(self):\n        \"\"\"Picks the correct scene for low, moderate, and high chance of rain, including the moderate/high boundary\"\"\"\n        if self.logic_snippet is None:\n            self.skipTest(\n                \"We couldn't find the provided drawing section marker in main.py. \"\n                \"Make sure you haven't edited or removed the '--- Provided ---' comment lines.\"\n            )\n\n        scenarios = [\n            (10, \"sunny\"),     # clearly low\n            (40, \"overcast\"),  # clearly moderate\n            (60, \"overcast\"),  # the boundary this level is actually about\n            (61, \"rainy\"),     # clearly high, just past the boundary\n            (90, \"rainy\"),     # clearly high\n        ]\n\n        mismatches = []\n        for chance_of_rain, expected_scene in scenarios:\n            actual = self._scene_for(chance_of_rain)\n            if actual != expected_scene:\n                mismatches.append(f\"chance_of_rain={chance_of_rain} -> expected scene='{expected_scene}', got '{actual}'\")\n\n        self.assertFalse(\n            mismatches,\n            \"The scene didn't match the expected chance-of-rain band for these values:\\n\" + \"\\n\".join(mismatches)\n        )",
      "folderId": "0",
      "active": false,
      "type": "validation"
    }
